--- abstract

DomainKeys Identified Mail v2 (DKIM2) requires any modifications made
to email messages by intermediaries to be recorded in "recipes".
Some originators wish to request that only very limited modifications be
made to their messages so that the recipient of the message can have some
assurance that the modifications were benign. This document introduces the
CanModify header field into which originator requests may be placed. It
then sets out a small set of modifications that are commonly applied
by intermediaries and assesses how benign they may actually be.

--- middle

# Introduction

DomainKeys Identified Mail v2 (DKIM2) requires any modifications made
to email messages by intermediaries to be recorded in "recipes"
(see [DKIM2SPEC]).
Some originators wish to request that only very limited modifications be
made to their messages so that the recipient of the message can have some
assurance that the modifications were benign.

# Terminology and definitions

{::boilerplate bcp14-tagged}

DKIM2 is designed to operate within the Internet Mail service, as
defined in [RFC5598].  Basic email terminology is taken from that
specification.

Syntax descriptions use Augmented BNF (ABNF) [RFC5234], together with
the %s"..." notation for case-sensitive strings defined in [RFC7405].

## Whitespace

There are two forms of whitespace used in this specification:

*  WSP represents simple whitespace, i.e. a space or a tab character
   (formal definition in [RFC5234]).

*  FWS is folding whitespace.  It allows multiple lines separated by
   CRLF followed by at least one whitespace, to be joined.

The formal ABNF for these are (WSP given for information only):

    WSP =   SP / HTAB
    FWS =   [*WSP CRLF] 1*WSP

The definition of FWS is identical to that in [RFC5322] except for
the exclusion of obs-FWS.

Other tokens not defined herein are imported from [RFC5234].  These
are intuitive primitives such as SP, HTAB, WSP, ALPHA, DIGIT, CRLF,
etc.

# The CanModify header field

A CanModify header field holds a comma-separated list of types
of modification that an email originator is content that intermediaries
can do to a message. Types can appear in any order, and SHOULD only
appear once. Unrecognized types are ignored.

The request is not valid if a message contains more than one CanModify
header field or the CanModify header field is not in conformance with this
specification.

The request is not valid if the current CanModify header field in the
message is not identical to any CanModify header field covered by the
i= DKIM2-Signature header field (viz it was not added at origination, or
has been added, removed or modified since). Alterations will be
apparent from inspecting the DKIM2 header recipes in the message.

The request is not valid if a message is not a Fully Verified DKIM2
Message (see [DKIM2SPEC] for how this is determined).

An absence of a CanModify header field means that no originator request
is being made. If the request is for no modifications whatsoever then use
"CanModify: none".

It is entirely a matter of local policy whether or not a receiver (or an
intermediary) takes any notice of requests, what it does when it can
determine that another system has modified the message in a way that
an originator has not permitted, and what action is taken
when there is an invalid request in the message.

ABNF:

    cm-field     = "CanModify:" [FWS] cm-request
                   *( [FWS] "," [FWS] cm-request )
    cm-request   = %s"none" / %s"bodywrap" / %s"subjectprefix" /
                   %s"alterfrom" / x-cm-request
    x-cm-request = 1*( ALPHA / DIGIT / "-" / "_" ) ; for future additions

## The "none" modification request

If used, "none" MUST be the only request made. It expresses a request by an
email originator for no modifications to be made to a message.

## The "bodywrap" modification

If used, "bodywrap" indicates that the originator is content for the original
message to be delivered as MIME part, optionally preceded or succeeded by
other MIME parts that provide headers or footers.

This is a benign change provided that the "header" and "footer" contain
simple text and URLs. A later version of this document will try and
tie this down more precisely.

## The "subjectprefix" modification

If used, "subjectprefix" indicates that the originator is content for the
Subject header field of the original message to be preceded by up
to 32 ASCII characters (e.g. "`[EXTERNAL]`" or "`[MAILINGLISTNAME]`").

This is a benign change provided that the receiver is viewing enough
characters from the original Subject header field to comprehend
what it originally said, and that important semantic information
does not appear towards the end of the Subject header field.

## The "alterfrom" modification

If used, "alterfrom" indicates that the originator is content for the From
header field of the original message to be replaced by a different
From header field.

This is benign if the receiver displays the original From header field,
and because DKIM2 insists that From header fields are Authentic there
are limits as to how inventive an intermediary could be in misleading
a recipient.

